---
source: disk/tests/help.rs
expression: "help_output(\"mount\")"
---
Mount a filesystem.

It can be used in four ways:

  mount                    List the mounted filesystems, like findmnt.
  mount -a                 Mount every entry in /etc/fstab that isn't
                           mounted yet, except noauto, swap and ignore
                           entries.
  mount TARGET             Look up TARGET in /etc/fstab and mount it as
                           configured there.
  mount SOURCE TARGET      Mount SOURCE on TARGET. SOURCE can be a device
                           path or a LABEL= or UUID= tag.

Not supported yet: --move, the --make-shared, --make-slave,
--make-private and --make-unbindable options, mount.FSTYPE helpers,
loop devices, writing mtab or utab, and -F (--fork).

Usage: mount [OPTIONS] [SPEC] [TARGET]

Arguments:
  [SPEC]
          Source to mount, such as a device, an NFS export or a tag. When it is the only argument,
          it is treated as the target instead

  [TARGET]
          Mount point

Options:
  -a, --all
          Mount every entry in fstab. Skips entries with the noauto option, entries of type swap or
          ignore, and entries whose target is already mounted. The -t and -O filters apply

  -t, --types <TYPE>
          Filesystem type, such as ext4, tmpfs or proc. Needed when mounting a source directly,
          unless an option like --bind is given. With -a, it limits which fstab entries are mounted.
          It takes a comma-separated list of types to include. If the first type starts with "no",
          the list is of types to skip instead (-a -t nonfs,cifs skips nfs and cifs)

  -O, --test-opts <LIST>
          With -a, only mount entries that match this comma-separated list of options. Each option
          must be present (name) or absent (noname), and an entry must match all of them

  -o, --options <LIST>
          Mount options, separated by commas. When the target is looked up in fstab, these are
          combined with the options there

  -r, --read-only
          Mount read-only (same as -o ro)

  -w, --rw
          Mount read-write (same as -o rw, and the default)

  -B, --bind
          Bind mount (MS_BIND). Needs both a source and a target

  -R, --rbind
          Recursive bind mount (MS_BIND and MS_REC)

      --remount
          Remount an already-mounted filesystem with new options

  -T, --fstab <FILE>
          Use FILE as the fstab instead of /etc/fstab

  -L, --label <LABEL>
          Mount the filesystem with this label (same as a LABEL= source)

  -U, --uuid <UUID>
          Mount the filesystem with this UUID (same as a UUID= source)

  -n, --no-mtab
          Don't write to /etc/mtab. This has no effect, since mtab is never written, but is accepted
          for compatibility

  -v, --verbose
          Print what is being done

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')
